1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:04,415
NARRATOR: Sixty thousand seats.
Every light switched on.

2
00:00:04,765 --> 00:00:07,162
And only one ticket sold. Yours.

3
00:00:08,362 --> 00:00:12,551
The biggest band on the planet. A
private concert. For one person.

4
00:00:12,901 --> 00:00:14,401
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Is all
of this... for me?

5
00:00:14,751 --> 00:00:16,103
MARCUS: All of it, Sir.

6
00:00:18,103 --> 00:00:21,779
NARRATOR: This is your day. Today, you
are the richest person on Earth.

7
00:00:22,129 --> 00:00:24,398
And at midnight... you
have to give it back.

8
00:00:26,598 --> 00:00:29,571
What if you were the richest
person on Earth... for one day?

9
00:00:32,671 --> 00:00:36,433
Six a.m. You wake up in a bed
bigger than most people's homes.

10
00:00:36,783 --> 00:00:40,203
You haven't touched anything. The
curtains open by themselves.

11
00:00:41,403 --> 00:00:46,189
MARCUS: Good morning, Sir. Breakfast is
ready. So are eleven people who need you.

12
00:00:46,539 --> 00:00:48,381
YOU (AS THE RICHEST):
Eleven? It's six o'clock.

13
00:00:48,731 --> 00:00:49,912
MARCUS: Twelve now.

14
00:00:50,712 --> 00:00:55,071
NARRATOR: And somewhere else, in a small room
in Lagos... the real one just woke up.

15
00:00:55,421 --> 00:00:57,903
In your bed. Under your
fan. To your alarm.

16
00:00:58,253 --> 00:01:00,394
Remember that. We're coming back for it.

17
00:01:01,594 --> 00:01:04,161
You brush your teeth. Two
minutes, like always.

18
00:01:04,511 --> 00:01:06,716
But your money doesn't
wait while you brush.

19
00:01:07,516 --> 00:01:10,531
Your fortune is about eight
hundred billion dollars.

20
00:01:10,881 --> 00:01:15,799
Even growing slowly, five percent a year,
it makes about seventy-six thousand

21
00:01:15,799 --> 00:01:17,651
dollars. Every single minute.

22
00:01:20,151 --> 00:01:24,233
By the time you spit, you've made more than
two teachers earn in a whole year.

23
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:26,703
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): ...I should
brush my teeth more often.

24
00:01:27,903 --> 00:01:31,259
NARRATOR: Your wardrobe. Forty
suits. All exactly the same.

25
00:01:31,609 --> 00:01:36,093
Because choosing what to wear is one
decision too many, when you have a thousand

26
00:01:36,093 --> 00:01:36,822
more waiting.

27
00:01:37,622 --> 00:01:40,552
ADA: Good morning, Sir. The chef
would like to know what you want.

28
00:01:40,902 --> 00:01:41,933
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Anything?

29
00:01:42,283 --> 00:01:43,400
ADA: Any country.

30
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,005
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Jollof rice. French
croissants. Japanese... everything.

31
00:01:48,355 --> 00:01:49,621
And pancakes.

32
00:01:49,971 --> 00:01:53,669
NARRATOR: Nobody says no to you.
Nobody has said no to you in years.

33
00:01:55,769 --> 00:01:57,013
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): What's for lunch?

34
00:01:57,363 --> 00:02:00,101
ADA: Wherever you like, Sir.
Paris is two hours away.

35
00:02:00,451 --> 00:02:02,101
YOU (AS THE RICHEST):
Then... lunch in Paris.

36
00:02:03,901 --> 00:02:07,258
NARRATOR: No queue. No security
line. No waiting at the gate.

37
00:02:07,608 --> 00:02:10,623
The plane leaves when you sit
down. Because it's yours.

38
00:02:12,723 --> 00:02:14,672
ADA: That island down there is yours, Sir.

39
00:02:15,022 --> 00:02:16,032
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Which one?

40
00:02:16,382 --> 00:02:18,651
ADA: The one on the left. And
the one on the right.

41
00:02:20,051 --> 00:02:22,618
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): This is the
best bread I have ever eaten.

42
00:02:22,968 --> 00:02:24,767
ADA: Would you like to buy the bakery?

43
00:02:25,117 --> 00:02:26,106
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): ...Can I?

44
00:02:26,456 --> 00:02:27,295
ADA: Done.

45
00:02:28,095 --> 00:02:30,193
Sir, we have to leave. London at three.

46
00:02:30,543 --> 00:02:32,023
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): I've had one bite.

47
00:02:32,373 --> 00:02:34,001
ADA: It was a very good bite.

48
00:02:34,351 --> 00:02:36,599
NARRATOR: You flew to Paris.
For eleven minutes.

49
00:02:37,399 --> 00:02:38,942
ADA: Sir... it's the President.

50
00:02:39,292 --> 00:02:40,238
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Of what?

51
00:02:40,588 --> 00:02:41,726
ADA: Does it matter?

52
00:02:42,526 --> 00:02:47,867
THE PRESIDENT (PHONE): My dear friend! I hope I'm not
disturbing you. I have a small favour to ask.

53
00:02:48,217 --> 00:02:52,235
A very small favour... with a
very large number attached.

54
00:02:53,035 --> 00:02:55,453
NARRATOR: A president.
Calling you. For a favour.

55
00:02:55,803 --> 00:02:59,988
And that's when you start to notice.
Everybody who calls you today... wants

56
00:02:59,988 --> 00:03:00,546
something.

57
00:03:02,046 --> 00:03:06,922
Back in the city, you always ride in the
middle car. There are always two more. Just

58
00:03:06,922 --> 00:03:07,387
in case.

59
00:03:08,187 --> 00:03:12,873
Then you smell it. A food cart on the
corner. Steam, and people laughing in the

60
00:03:12,873 --> 00:03:13,229
queue.

61
00:03:13,579 --> 00:03:15,975
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Stop the
car. I want one of those.

62
00:03:16,775 --> 00:03:19,556
NARRATOR: You haven't eaten from a
street cart in twenty years.

63
00:03:19,906 --> 00:03:22,537
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Just me,
Marcus. It's two minutes away.

64
00:03:23,337 --> 00:03:24,945
MARCUS: Not today, Sir.

65
00:03:25,295 --> 00:03:27,073
YOU (AS THE RICHEST):
Marcus. It's a sandwich.

66
00:03:27,423 --> 00:03:30,993
MARCUS: Last time it was a
sandwich. Then it was a crowd.

67
00:03:32,293 --> 00:03:37,075
NARRATOR: Keeping one person this safe costs a
fortune. One tech company spent about

68
00:03:37,075 --> 00:03:41,793
twenty-seven million dollars, in a single
year, just protecting its boss.

69
00:03:42,143 --> 00:03:45,500
You can buy anything on this
street. Except a walk down it.

70
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:50,254
So you get back in the car. And you tell
yourself that was the hard part.

71
00:03:50,604 --> 00:03:53,193
It wasn't. The hard part
hasn't happened yet.

72
00:03:54,593 --> 00:03:57,843
Your afternoon arrives in
blocks. Fifteen minutes each.

73
00:03:58,193 --> 00:04:03,306
Bosses of giant companies work around
sixty-two hours a week. Nearly three-quarters

74
00:04:03,306 --> 00:04:04,600
of it... in meetings.

75
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:08,981
ADA: Four hundred jobs in
Brazil. Yes or no?

76
00:04:09,331 --> 00:04:10,085
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Yes.

77
00:04:10,435 --> 00:04:11,658
ADA: The new factory?

78
00:04:12,008 --> 00:04:12,762
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Yes.

79
00:04:13,112 --> 00:04:14,037
ADA: Your lunch?

80
00:04:14,387 --> 00:04:15,290
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): ...Later.

81
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:18,828
ADA: And the old factory in
Ohio. Keep it open?

82
00:04:19,178 --> 00:04:20,017
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): ...No.

83
00:04:20,367 --> 00:04:25,632
NARRATOR: One word. Somewhere, four hundred
kitchens go quiet. And you decided it between two

84
00:04:25,632 --> 00:04:26,711
meetings, hungry.

85
00:04:28,111 --> 00:04:32,065
Every yes changes somebody's life.
And lunch... never happens.

86
00:04:33,465 --> 00:04:37,461
Then your phone rings. Not a
president. Somebody you actually know.

87
00:04:37,811 --> 00:04:40,208
OLD FRIEND: Ehh! Look at you!
Remember me? Chemistry class?

88
00:04:40,558 --> 00:04:42,656
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): You set
the teacher's wig on fire!

89
00:04:43,456 --> 00:04:44,573
OLD FRIEND: It was an accident!

90
00:04:44,923 --> 00:04:46,658
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): It was a WIG!

91
00:04:47,008 --> 00:04:49,490
NARRATOR: It's the first time
you've really laughed all day.

92
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:55,225
OLD FRIEND: So... listen. About my business. I just
need a small push. You won't even notice it.

93
00:04:58,225 --> 00:05:02,179
NARRATOR: After that, you can't stop seeing
it. Every smile comes with a hand.

94
00:05:02,529 --> 00:05:06,590
And you start to wonder which of them would
still call... if you had nothing at all.

95
00:05:07,990 --> 00:05:09,960
Evening. A party in your honour.

96
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:14,989
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Why did the banker break up with
the calculator? ...It just didn't count on him.

97
00:05:15,339 --> 00:05:19,404
NARRATOR: It's the worst joke you know. Two
hundred people laugh like it's the best thing

98
00:05:19,404 --> 00:05:20,381
they've ever heard.

99
00:05:21,181 --> 00:05:23,599
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): So a man
walks into a bank, and...

100
00:05:23,949 --> 00:05:27,135
NARRATOR: So you try a joke with no
ending. They laugh harder.

101
00:05:30,035 --> 00:05:34,245
And then it's night, and the moment from the
start of this story finally happens.

102
00:05:34,595 --> 00:05:39,061
Every light. Every firework. The whole
world's favourite song. Just for you.

103
00:05:41,461 --> 00:05:43,282
YOU (AS THE RICHEST): Did
you see that? Did you—

104
00:05:44,382 --> 00:05:47,419
NARRATOR: Sixty thousand seats.
And nobody to turn to.

105
00:05:50,619 --> 00:05:54,871
You go home. Forty rooms. Three
chefs, asleep. One fridge light.

106
00:05:55,221 --> 00:05:59,730
The richest person on Earth eats dinner
standing up. Out of a cereal box.

107
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:03,828
It is very, very quiet up here.

108
00:06:06,928 --> 00:06:08,919
And then the clock does what clocks do.

109
00:06:11,119 --> 00:06:12,727
Midnight. You give it back.

110
00:06:13,077 --> 00:06:16,113
But the real question isn't what
you did with his day...

111
00:06:16,463 --> 00:06:18,028
...it's what he did with yours.

112
00:06:19,928 --> 00:06:22,751
MAMA: Are you going to sleep
until Christmas? Get up!

113
00:06:23,101 --> 00:06:25,605
NARRATOR: You're home. Your
room. Your fan. Your mother.

114
00:06:26,405 --> 00:06:28,481
MAMA: And what happened to you yesterday?

115
00:06:28,831 --> 00:06:30,183
YOU: Yesterday?

116
00:06:30,533 --> 00:06:33,015
MAMA: Oh, you don't
remember? Let me remind you.

117
00:06:33,815 --> 00:06:37,065
NARRATOR: Yesterday, the richest person
on Earth went to your market.

118
00:06:37,415 --> 00:06:39,513
IYA OSUN: Five oranges.
Two thousand naira.

119
00:06:39,863 --> 00:06:42,622
YOU: Wonderful! I will give
you four thousand.

120
00:06:42,972 --> 00:06:44,558
IYA OSUN: Ehn? Is that how haggling works?

121
00:06:45,358 --> 00:06:47,371
YOU: Thank you, madam. Truly.

122
00:06:47,721 --> 00:06:51,376
NARRATOR: He paid double. And thanked
her. She is still telling everybody.

123
00:06:52,676 --> 00:06:56,651
He took the bus. Squashed between
twelve strangers and one chicken.

124
00:06:57,001 --> 00:07:01,787
Nobody looked at him. Nobody wanted
anything from him. He loved every minute.

125
00:07:03,587 --> 00:07:05,195
MAMA: Eat. You're too thin.

126
00:07:05,545 --> 00:07:09,725
NARRATOR: At dinner, your mother gave him the
biggest piece of meat. Without being asked.

127
00:07:09,725 --> 00:07:11,312
Without wanting anything back.

128
00:07:12,112 --> 00:07:15,682
The richest person on Earth didn't
say anything for a long time.

129
00:07:18,082 --> 00:07:21,481
Then he washed every plate.
Nobody asked. He was humming.

130
00:07:21,831 --> 00:07:24,441
MAMA: Who are you, and what have
you done with my child?

131
00:07:25,241 --> 00:07:26,443
LITTLE SISTER: I win! Again!

132
00:07:26,793 --> 00:07:28,571
NARRATOR: He let your sister win at cards.

133
00:07:28,921 --> 00:07:31,297
LITTLE SISTER: You let me win. I
know you did. I still won.

134
00:07:32,097 --> 00:07:35,859
NARRATOR: And just before midnight, he
stood at your window. For a long time.

135
00:07:36,209 --> 00:07:38,136
Like someone who didn't want to leave.

136
00:07:40,936 --> 00:07:45,082
MAMA: You were strange yesterday. Polite. You
washed the plates without being told.

137
00:07:45,432 --> 00:07:46,677
Do it again.

138
00:07:47,477 --> 00:07:50,257
NARRATOR: So. One day as the
richest person on Earth.

139
00:07:50,607 --> 00:07:52,087
Would you have given it back?

140
00:07:55,187 --> 00:07:57,925
Next time, you borrow somebody else's day.

141
00:07:58,275 --> 00:08:03,167
Ninety thousand people. One penalty. And
every one of them is screaming your name.

142
00:08:05,367 --> 00:08:08,489
Tell us in the comments: whose
day should you borrow next?
